 "mod-1.title": "Linux and Systems",
 "mod-1.tagline": "The ground where every incident is investigated.",
 "mod-1.overview": "Linux is the non-negotiable foundation of the career. Here you learn to navigate the filesystem with intent, really understand permissions (including the ones that cause deploy failures), control processes and services with systemd, read logs to find the root cause, and diagnose CPU, memory and disk under pressure. The module ends with your own runbook — a document on-call operators actually use.",
 "mod-1.objectives": "Navigate, search, and inspect files efficiently | Understand and correct permissions and ownership | Control processes and services with systemd | Investigate logs with journalctl and /var/log | Diagnose CPU, memory, and disk saturation",
 "mod-1.prerequisites": "Module 0 completed | Working Linux environment",
 "mod-1.delivery": "Runbook with 30 commented commands and your own systemd service, controlled and resilient to reboot.",
 "mod-1.checklist": "Runbook in docs/runbook-linux.md with 30 commands and their purpose | systemd service created, enabled on boot and with automatic restart | Log querying by unit and by time interval mastered | Full disk diagnosis performed with du/df | User without sudo created to run the application",
 "mod-1.troubleshooting": "Full disk in production: df -h shows 100% on /, the application stops writing, and the database rejects connections. Investigation: du -xh --max-depth=1 / to find the culprit directory, usually /var/log or old Docker images. Immediate fix: rotate/clear logs and prune images; definitive fix: logrotate and 80% alert.",
 "mod-1.interviewQuestions": "What do you look at first when a server is slow? | Explain the difference between load average and CPU usage. | How would you make a service automatically restart after failure?",
 "mod-1.capstone": "Prepare the host that will host the CloudShop API: dedicated user, systemd service, and logs under control.",
 "l-1-1.title": "Filesystem and navigating with intent",
 "l-1-1.whyItMatters": "In an incident, navigation speed determines how many minutes the service is down. Someone who searches for a file by clicking misses the window.",
 "l-1-1.commonMistake": "Using cat on huge files and freezing the SSH session during a crisis.",
 "l-1-1.productionTip": "Prefer less +G and tail -f; and always confirm with stat who owns the configuration file.",
 "l-1-1.interviewQuestion": "Where would you look for the configuration and logs of an unknown service on a server?",
 "l-1-1.capstone": "Define /opt/cloudshop for the application and /var/log/cloudshop for the logs.",
 "l-1-1.codeNote0": "Avoid recursive grep on / as root: besides being slow, it can expose sensitive content in the terminal history.",
 "l-1-1.q0": "Where are system configuration files conventionally located?",
 "l-1-1.q0exp": "/etc centralizes static configuration for the system and installed services.",
 "l-1-2.title": "Permissions, users and groups without rote memorization",
 "l-1-2.whyItMatters": "Permission errors appear as mysterious deployment failures, uploads that don't save, and services that don't start. Diagnosing them in seconds is a differentiator.",
 "l-1-2.commonMistake": "Solving 'permission denied' with chmod -R 777 instead of fixing the owner.",
 "l-1-2.productionTip": "Files with credentials: 600 or 640, owned by the service, and never versioned in Git.",
 "l-1-2.securityAlert": "Never give sudo without a password to an application user; if it's compromised, the entire server goes down.",
 "l-1-2.interviewQuestion": "An upload fails with permission denied. How would you investigate in order?",
 "l-1-2.capstone": "Create the cloudshop user who will execute the API and own the application directories.",
 "l-1-2.codeNote0": "chmod 777 on an application directory is a security flaw: any local user can replace your binary or script.",
 "l-1-2.q0": "What does 750 mean on a directory?",
 "l-1-2.q0opts": "Everyone can write | Owner reads/writes/accesses, group reads and accesses, others nothing | Read-only for everyone | Owner reads only",
 "l-1-2.q0exp": "7 = rwx for the owner, 5 = r-x for the group, 0 = nothing for others.",
 "l-1-3.title": "Processes, signals and systemd in practice",
