 "l-10-2.body": "Prometheus scrapes metrics and stores time series. In PromQL, the rate function calculates the per-second rate of counters, histogram_quantile estimates percentiles from buckets, and aggregations by label allow observing behavior by route or service. | A good alert is one that requires human action and describes the user impact: '5xx error rate above 2% for 5 minutes' is worth waking someone up; 'CPU at 85%' usually isn't, because it can be healthy. Use for to avoid alerting on transient spikes and severity consistent with urgency. | In Alertmanager, group related alerts, define inhibition (don't alert on symptoms when the cause has already fired), and route by severity. Alert fatigue is an engineering failure: if the team ignores a notification, the alerting system is broken. | <h3>1. How Prometheus works</h3><p>It <strong>pulls</strong> (scrapes) metrics from <code>/metrics</code> endpoints at intervals, stores them in a time series database, and evaluates alert rules. In Kubernetes, kube-prometheus-stack discovers targets via <code>ServiceMonitor</code>.</p> | <h3>2. Metric types</h3><ul><li><strong>Counter</strong>: only increases (total requests) — always use with <code>rate()</code></li><li><strong>Gauge</strong>: goes up and down (memory in use)</li><li><strong>Histogram</strong>: distributes values into buckets to calculate percentiles</li></ul> | <h3>3. Essential PromQL</h3><p><code>rate(x[5m])</code> gives the per-second rate; <code>sum by (route)</code> aggregates; <code>histogram_quantile(0.95, …)</code> calculates the p95. Average latency hides problems — alert on percentiles.</p> | <h3>4. Alerts worth on-call duty</h3><p>Alert on <strong>symptoms</strong> that the user experiences (error, slowness), not on causes (high CPU). Every alert needs: <code>for</code> to avoid noise, severity, and a link to a runbook. If no one needs to act, it's not an alert, it's a dashboard.</p> | <h3>5. Alertmanager</h3><p>Groups, deduplicates, silences, and routes: critical goes to on-call (PagerDuty/Opsgenie), warning goes to chat.</p>",
 "l-10-2.glossaryTerms": "rate | burn rate | Scrape | Counter | Percentile p95",
 "l-10-2.glossaryDefs": "PromQL function that calculates the per-second rate of a counter. | The speed at which the error budget is consumed relative to what is allowed. | Periodic collection of metrics exposed by a target. | A metric that only increases; handled with rate(). | The value below which 95% of measurements fall.",
 "l-10-2.codeLabels": "Essential PromQL for CloudShop | Alert rules by symptom | Everyday PromQL queries | Alert rule with runbook",
 "l-10-2.q1": "How to query a counter correctly?",
 "l-10-2.q1opts": "Raw value | With rate() or increase() | With avg() | With max()",
 "l-10-2.q1exp": "The raw value only grows; what matters is the rate of change.",
 "l-10-2.q2": "What is 'for: 10m' used for in an alert?",
 "l-10-2.q2opts": "Repeat every 10 min | Only fire if the condition lasts for 10 minutes | Delete after 10 min | Nothing",
 "l-10-2.q2exp": "Reduces false alarms caused by short spikes.",
 "l-10-3.body": "A dashboard is not a gallery of charts: it's a decision-making tool. The first panel answers 'is everything okay?' with error rate, p95, and throughput. Subsequent panels help locate the problem: by route, by dependency, by resource. If a panel is never used in an incident, it only takes up space. | Loki indexes labels and stores compressed content, making it cheap and integrated into the same label model as Prometheus. With LogQL, you filter by stream and content, extract fields from JSON, and even generate metrics from logs. | The biggest gain is correlation: from the latency panel, jump to the logs for that interval and service in two clicks. Standardizing structured logs with requestId and traceId is what allows closing the metric → log → trace cycle. | <h3>1. Dashboard that helps during an incident</h3><p>Top: the golden signals of the service. Middle: dependencies (database, queue, payment gateway). Bottom: resources (CPU, memory, Pods). Someone opening it at 3 AM needs to understand in 10 seconds if the problem is here.</p> | <h3>2. Variables and dashboards as code</h3><p>Variables (<code>$namespace</code>, <code>$service</code>) prevent having one panel per service. Store dashboards as JSON in Git and provision them via ConfigMap or Terraform — no lost clicks.</p> | <h3>3. Loki: cheap logs</h3><p>Loki indexes only labels (namespace, app), not the text — that's why it's cheap. Promtail or Grafana Alloy collect logs from nodes. LogQL queries filter by labels and then by content.</p> | <h3>4. From metric to log</h3><p>When you see an error spike on the graph, click and open the logs for the same interval and service; with trace_id in the log, jump directly to the trace in Tempo.</p>",
 "l-10-3.glossaryTerms": "LogQL | structured log | Loki | LogQL | Provisioning",
 "l-10-3.glossaryDefs": "Loki's query language, inspired by PromQL. | A log emitted as an object (JSON) with queryable fields. | A log system that indexes only labels, reducing cost. | Loki's log query language. | Loading dashboards and data sources from versioned files.",
 "l-10-3.codeLabels": "LogQL queries | Structured log in the application | LogQL: find and count errors",
 "l-10-3.q1": "Why is Loki cheaper than solutions that index all text?",
