 "l-11-5.body": "A DevOps portfolio is not a list of technologies: it's evidence of operation. Organized repositories, READMEs with diagrams, visible and green pipelines, dashboards with screenshots, a real postmortem, and ADRs. This is worth more than ten unpracticed certificates. | In your resume, each line should have a verb, scope, and measured result: 'reduced build time from 22 to 4 minutes with dependency caching and multi-stage builds' communicates competence; 'knowledge of Docker' communicates nothing. Numbers from your own project are legitimate and verifiable. | In the interview, the script is predictable: a Linux/network problem, a container/Kubernetes problem, a pipeline problem, a cloud/security problem, and a behavioral question about an incident. Practice aloud using CloudShop as a reference, always in three parts: situation, what you did, measured result. | <h3>1. Portfolio that proves</h3><p>A complete project (CloudShop) is worth more than ten tutorials: infra with Terraform, cluster, pipeline, GitOps, observability, SLO, and a simulated postmortem. Show numbers.</p> | <h3>2. Impact-oriented resume</h3><p>Formula: <em>verb + what you did + tool + result</em>. \"Reduced deploy time from 40 to 8 minutes with layer caching and parallel pipeline jobs in GitHub Actions.\"</p> | <h3>3. Technical interview</h3><p>Expect questions about fundamentals (DNS, TLS, Linux), scenario (\"the site is slow, what do you do?\"), and design (\"design the deployment of an API with zero downtime\"). Think aloud, ask context questions, and talk about trade-offs.</p> | <h3>4. STAR Method</h3><p>For behavioral questions: Situation, Task, Action, and Result. Use the incident from your project as a story.</p> | <h3>5. Keep evolving</h3><p>Useful certifications (CKA, AWS SAA, Terraform Associate), open-source contributions, and writing about what you've learned keep you visible.</p>",
 "l-11-5.glossaryTerms": "portfolio | STAR | STAR | Trade-off",
 "l-11-5.glossaryDefs": "Set of verifiable evidence of what you know how to operate. | Response structure: situation, task, action, and result. | Situation, Task, Action, Result: structure for behavioral responses. | A conscious choice between the advantages and disadvantages of alternatives.",
 "l-11-5.codeLabels": "Application Checklist | Response script: 'the site is slow'",
